<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastphalia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ostfalen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eastphalia (Ostfalen) was the eastern division of Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> region and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Angria</a> it made up the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. The name survives as a historical and dialect region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p></div>
